<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Atisha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/982">982</a>–<a href="/years/1054">1054</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Atiśa, Atisha Dipankara Shrijnana</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Atisha Dipankara Shrijnana was a Buddhist teacher from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> who taught at the monasteries of the Pala Empire, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikramashila">Vikramashila</a>. Invited by the rulers of Guge, he arrived in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> in 1042 and spent the rest of his life in Tibet, writing the Lamp for the Path to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>. His disciples founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kadam-school">Kadam school</a>, and his work was central to the revival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> in Tibet.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/atisha.jpg" alt="Atisha" width="320" height="442" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown不具名 [Tibet (a Kadampa monastery)] · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Atisha.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Atiśa was born as Candragarbha about 982, the second of three sons in a ruling family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, most probably in the village of Vajrayogini in Vikrampura, one of the capitals of the Pala Empire. His father was a king known as Kalyānaśrī and his mother was Prabhavati Sri. As a young man he began to study tantra in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajgir">Rajgir</a> under a monk named Rāhulaguhyavajra, who initiated him into Hevajra. He then studied for seven years under a master named Avadhūtipā. According to Tibetan sources he was ordained at the age of twenty-eight by the abbot Śīlarakṣita at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodh-gaya">Bodh Gaya</a>, and he studied almost all the Buddhist and non-Buddhist schools of his time.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Atiśa became a senior scholar at the monastery of Vikramaśīla when it had no more than one hundred ordained monks. Tibetan sources record that he spent twelve years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a> in the empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a>, and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1025. He was appointed steward or abbot at Vikramaśīla, and he is also said to have nourished Odantapuri. The king of the Tibetan polity of Guge, Lha bla ma Ye shes 'od, sent missions to Vikramaśīla to invite scholars who could teach a pure form of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>. Atiśa left Vikramaśīla in 1040, with the abbot Ratnakara's permission on condition that he return in three years.</p><p>He travelled through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> with Naktso and Gya Lōtsawa to Tolung, the capital of the Purang Kingdom, and spent three years there. In Tolung he met Dromtön, who became his main disciple.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>At Tolung Atiśa compiled his most influential work, the Bodhipathapradīpa, or Lamp for the Path to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>. This short text of sixty-seven verses sets out the whole Buddhist path in terms of the three vehicles, Hīnayāna, Mahāyāna and Vajrayāna. It became the model for later texts of the Lamrim genre, the Stages of the Path, and was the basis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsongkhapa">Tsongkhapa</a>'s writings on the subject. Atiśa and Dromtön are regarded as founders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kadam-school">Kadam school</a>, one of the New Translation schools of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a>. In the 14th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelug">Gelug</a> tradition adopted its teachings and absorbed its monasteries. His other writings include the Satyadvayavatara and the Madhyamakaratnapradipa.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Atiśa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Scholars trained in Pala monasteries carried Indian Buddhist texts and practices abroad, and the most celebrated of them, <b>Atisha Dipankara Shrijnana</b>, left Vikramashila around 1040 and spent his last years in Tibet, where his teaching helped shape the later spread of Buddhism and the Kadam school.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pala-empire">Pala Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>They founded Tholing monastery in 996, sent the translator Rinchen Zangpo to Kashmir and invited the Indian teacher <b>Atisha</b>, who arrived in 1042, beginning the later diffusion of Buddhism in Tibet.</blockquote><a href="/state/guge">Guge</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Bikrampur, in what is now <a href="/countries/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a>. Died in Nyêtang town, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=23.55,90.55&amp;year=1028" aria-label="Bikrampur on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6965 1295 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Atisha"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7415 1565h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7415 1565h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=23.55,90.55&amp;year=1028">What was here in 1028? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
